WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.269
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:08.572
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.872 --> 00:00:16.227
Unit forty-five: Poverty and Development. In this unit, you will discuss how countries reduce poverty.

4
00:00:17.217 --> 00:00:18.311
poverty line.

5
00:00:19.011 --> 00:00:23.588
poverty line means the minimum income needed to meet basic needs.

6
00:00:23.888 --> 00:00:27.594
Millions of people have risen above the poverty line.

7
00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:31.225
Your turn. Say: poverty line.

8
00:00:33.925 --> 00:00:35.030
poverty line.

9
00:00:36.017 --> 00:00:37.494
extreme poverty.

10
00:00:38.194 --> 00:00:41.993
extreme poverty means living on less than about two dollars a day.

11
00:00:42.293 --> 00:00:47.427
Extreme poverty has fallen sharply since 1990.

12
00:00:48.127 --> 00:00:51.441
Your turn. Say: extreme poverty.

13
00:00:54.141 --> 00:00:55.607
extreme poverty.

14
00:00:56.617 --> 00:00:57.954
microfinance.

15
00:00:58.654 --> 00:01:03.649
microfinance means small loans for people without access to normal banks.

16
00:01:03.949 --> 00:01:07.214
Microfinance helped her open a small shop.

17
00:01:07.914 --> 00:01:11.159
Your turn. Say: microfinance.

18
00:01:13.859 --> 00:01:15.185
microfinance.

19
00:01:16.183 --> 00:01:17.765
social safety net.

20
00:01:18.465 --> 00:01:23.750
social safety net means government support that protects people from falling into poverty.

21
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.687
A strong social safety net helps families during crises.

22
00:01:28.387 --> 00:01:31.585
Your turn. Say: social safety net.

23
00:01:34.285 --> 00:01:35.855
social safety net.

24
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:38.710
human development index.

25
00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:45.891
human development index means a UN measure combining health, education and income.

26
00:01:46.191 --> 00:01:50.281
Cambodia's human development index has risen steadily.

27
00:01:50.981 --> 00:01:55.223
Your turn. Say: human development index.

28
00:01:57.923 --> 00:02:00.051
human development index.

29
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.887
conditional cash transfer.

30
00:02:03.587 --> 00:02:10.997
conditional cash transfer means money given to poor families if they meet conditions like school attendance.

31
00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:14.631
Conditional cash transfers increased school enrolment.

32
00:02:15.331 --> 00:02:19.156
Your turn. Say: conditional cash transfer.

33
00:02:21.856 --> 00:02:23.751
conditional cash transfer.

34
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.099
rural development.

35
00:02:26.799 --> 00:02:31.074
rural development means improving life and income in the countryside.

36
00:02:31.374 --> 00:02:34.245
Roads and electricity drive rural development.

37
00:02:34.945 --> 00:02:38.236
Your turn. Say: rural development.

38
00:02:40.936 --> 00:02:42.123
rural development.

39
00:02:43.117 --> 00:02:44.466
inclusive growth.

40
00:02:45.166 --> 00:02:50.115
inclusive growth means economic growth that benefits all groups in society.

41
00:02:50.415 --> 00:02:53.296
Inclusive growth reduces inequality.

42
00:02:53.996 --> 00:02:56.893
Your turn. Say: inclusive growth.

43
00:02:59.593 --> 00:03:00.884
inclusive growth.

44
00:03:01.883 --> 00:03:04.069
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:04.369 --> 00:03:08.690
Rithy, Cambodia's poverty rate has fallen dramatically, hasn't it?

46
00:03:09.983 --> 00:03:16.838
Yes. Measured against the national poverty line, it has more than halved since the early 2000s.

47
00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.197
Impressive. What do you think made the difference?

48
00:03:22.483 --> 00:03:28.746
Rapid growth, mainly. Combined with better roads, it transformed rural development.

49
00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:32.108
But growth alone isn't enough, is it?

50
00:03:33.417 --> 00:03:38.728
No. Without a social safety net, families can fall back into poverty quickly.

51
00:03:40.017 --> 00:03:45.688
What about microfinance? Once seen as a miracle, it's now more controversial.

52
00:03:46.983 --> 00:03:52.027
Because of debt. If poorly regulated, small loans can become heavy burdens.

53
00:03:53.317 --> 00:03:57.661
Grammar focus: Participle and verbless clauses (mastery).

54
00:03:57.961 --> 00:04:01.528
Reduced clauses make your English concise and sophisticated.

55
00:04:01.828 --> 00:04:08.149
Measured against the poverty line, it has halved. If poorly regulated, loans can become burdens.

56
00:04:08.449 --> 00:04:12.504
Once seen as a miracle, microfinance is now debated.

57
00:04:12.804 --> 00:04:17.961
These reduced clauses are hallmarks of sophisticated academic and journalistic writing.

58
00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:22.473
Now read and listen. Escaping Poverty.

59
00:04:22.773 --> 00:04:28.047
Over the past three decades, extreme poverty worldwide has fallen dramatically.

60
00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:34.368
Driven largely by economic growth in Asia, more than a billion people have escaped it.

61
00:04:35.683 --> 00:04:40.771
Cambodia's human development index has also risen steadily over this period.

62
00:04:42.083 --> 00:04:48.402
Investment in rural development, such as roads and electricity, connected farmers to markets.

63
00:04:49.717 --> 00:04:56.558
Conditional cash transfers, first tested in Latin America, have encouraged families to keep children in school.

64
00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:04.412
Microfinance, once celebrated as a solution, now faces criticism over rising household debt.

65
00:05:05.717 --> 00:05:11.373
Economists increasingly argue that inclusive growth and strong social safety nets are essential.

66
00:05:12.683 --> 00:05:18.236
Measured over a lifetime, education remains one of the most reliable routes out of poverty.

67
00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:22.226
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:22.526 --> 00:05:25.976
Small loans for people without access to banks are blank.

69
00:05:26.276 --> 00:05:31.126
A, microfinance. B, poverty line. C, inclusive growth.

70
00:05:35.117 --> 00:05:38.242
The answer is A: microfinance.

71
00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:43.537
blank by many experts, the plan was finally approved.

72
00:05:43.837 --> 00:05:47.990
A, Supported. B, Supporting. C, Support.

73
00:05:51.983 --> 00:05:54.215
The answer is A: Supported.

74
00:05:55.717 --> 00:05:58.123
blank in doubt, ask your teacher.

75
00:05:58.423 --> 00:06:01.437
A, When. B, Being. C, Had.

76
00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.682
The answer is A: When.

77
00:06:09.183 --> 00:06:12.797
Growth that benefits all groups in society is blank.

78
00:06:13.097 --> 00:06:18.422
A, inclusive growth. B, rural development. C, extreme poverty.

79
00:06:22.417 --> 00:06:25.322
The answer is A: inclusive growth.

80
00:06:26.817 --> 00:06:31.243
According to the text, what is one of the most reliable routes out of poverty?

81
00:06:31.543 --> 00:06:35.846
A, microfinance. B, education. C, debt.

82
00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:42.627
The answer is B: education.

83
00:06:44.117 --> 00:06:49.184
Now it's your turn. Part one: What is the most effective way to reduce poverty?

84
00:06:54.484 --> 00:07:01.569
Part two: Use a reduced clause: propose two strategies and evaluate one risk.

85
00:07:07.869 --> 00:07:21.716
Given the evidence, I'd prioritise education and rural infrastructure. Combined, they raise productivity and connect people to markets. If poorly managed, however, loans for these projects could create long-term debt.

86
00:07:22.016 --> 00:07:24.375
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:25.383 --> 00:07:30.927
Great work today! You learned eight new words and participle and verbless clauses (mastery).

88
00:07:31.227 --> 00:07:35.098
Today's value is kindness. Be kind to everyone.

89
00:07:35.398 --> 00:07:39.896
See you in Unit forty-six. Goodbye!

90
00:07:40.196 --> 00:07:43.603
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

